*** src/sem/examples/paper/StorageCopyMappingFlagged.maude
***
*** StorageCopyMapping.maude's program, run by the sem/ layer on the OTHER
*** storage model. Same statements, same derivation notation, same answers --
*** reached by asking a different question at the copy leaf.
***
***     StorageCopyMapping.maude   ../../../StorageCopy.maude   the FIELD's sort
***     this file                  ../../../StorageFlag.maude   the VALUE at the path
***
*** That file's block C already steps `find` in both models. This one is the
*** other half: the whole STATEMENT derivation, in a session whose storage model
*** is the flagged one.
***
*** HOW THE SWITCH IS DONE, and why it is a separate file. ../../Config.maude
*** loads one storage model for the whole session (line 26), and the variants
*** all define the module STORAGE, so two of them can never be in scope at once
*** -- that is the repo's swap idiom, and it is why this is a file over rather
*** than a block. Here the chain is loaded as usual and STORAGE is then
*** REDEFINED as a wrapper over STORAGE-FLAG:
***
***     load Domain .                      *** the sem/ layer, on StorageCopy
***     sload ../../../StorageFlag .
***     fmod STORAGE{Field :: TRIV} is pr STORAGE-FLAG{Field} . endfm
***
*** Maude reparses every importer of STORAGE -- SOL-CONFIG, SOL-EXPR, SOL-STMT
*** and the rest -- against the new one, which it announces as an Advisory and
*** not a Warning, so run-tests.sh stays green. The sem/ layer needs no port.
*** STORAGE-FLAG is missing exactly two of STORAGE's operators, storeSt and
*** selectSt: sem/ never names selectSt at all, and names storeSt only in two
*** comments in Contract.maude.
***
*** THE LAYOUT IS THE INPUT. The flagged model has no field sorts to read
*** map-ness off, so a mapping slot must be DECLARED, and the sem/ layer has no
*** notion of a declaration: ../../Steps.maude starts every goal at mtSt. An
*** initial update supplies it instead --
***
***     red steps({ storage := layout } < … >) .
***
*** -- which is a plain goal, not a new mechanism: `fill` applies `storage := S`
*** and takes the entry snapshot from it, the same convention Hoare.maude's
*** `[ st ]< … >` uses. Block C below is what happens without it.
***
*** WHAT EACH BLOCK SHOWS
***   A  the derivation, arrow for arrow with StorageCopyMapping.maude's A
***   B  the same claim, written out and CHECKED
***   C  the same program with NO layout: the mapping IS copied, 'b := 11
***
*** A ZERO-INIT OVERLAP to know about. ../../Config.maude:66-74 gives find(mtSt,
*** …) a sort-directed default -- a PrimField reads defaultValue, a RefField
*** reads mtSt -- and STORAGE-FLAG:120 gives it an untyped one, find(mtSt,
*** fldsN) = 0, deliberately non-owise. Both are in scope here and they overlap
*** on every path, agreeing at a primitive and disagreeing at a reference,
*** where Config says mtSt and the flagged model says 0. Nothing below depends
*** on the difference -- v→st casts a primitive to mtSt on the way down, which
*** is exactly the case the flagged model's own zero-init is written for -- but
*** a file that reads an unwritten struct location for its VALUE rather than to
*** descend through it would be reading an unspecified choice between two
*** equations, and should not be written against this session.

load Domain .
sload ../../../StorageFlag .

*** the switch: STORAGE is now the value-directed model
fmod STORAGE{Field :: TRIV} is
    pr STORAGE-FLAG{Field} .
endfm

*** As in StorageCopyMapping.maude, this is the module Steps.maude reflects, so
*** $ledger2 and `layout` are declared INSIDE it: a constant added from outside
*** does not exist in the module metaRewrite runs in.
fmod PAPER-FLAG-DOMAIN{Field :: TRIV} is
    pr PAPER-DOMAIN{Field} .

    op $ledger2 : -> RefField .
    eq defaultValue($ledger2) = mtSt .

    op $ledger2 : -> Root .
    var Clv : Cells .
    eq lower($ledger2, Clv) = sloc($ledger2) .

    *** the contract's storage layout: every mapping slot, with its entry
    *** template. In StorageCopyMapping.maude this information is carried by
    *** $balances being declared at sort MapField; here it is a term.
    op layout : -> Struct .
    eq layout = decl(decl(mtSt, $ledger $balances, 0), $ledger2 $balances, 0) .
endfm

fmod PAPER-FLAG-STEPS{Field :: TRIV} is
    pr PAPER-FLAG-DOMAIN{Field} .
    pr SOL-STEPS{Field} .
    eq theModule = 'PAPER-FLAG-DOMAIN .
endfm

*** ---- A  the derivation ---------------------------------------------------
*** Compare StorageCopyMapping.maude's A: same five statements, same five ~>,
*** same 'b := 0 || 'n := 5. The storage terms differ only by the layout they
*** are built over, and by the copied subtree carrying the source's declared
*** slot save(mtSt, $balances, mapOf(0)) where the sorted model has bare mtSt.
red in PAPER-FLAG-STEPS :
steps({ storage := layout }
      < $ledger . $nonce = # 5 ;  $ledger . $balances [ # 1 ] = # 11 ;
        $ledger2 = $ledger ;
        var 'n = $ledger2 . $nonce ;
        var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >) .
*** {storage := layout}< … > ~> {storage := save(layout, $ledger $nonce, 5)}< … >
***   ~> {storage := L2}< ($ledger2).Root = ($ledger).Root ; … >
***   ~> {storage := save(L2, $ledger2, SRC)}< var 'n = … ; var 'b = … ; >
***   ~> {storage := save(L2, $ledger2, SRC) || 'n := 5}< var 'b = … ; >
***   ~> {storage := save(L2, $ledger2, SRC) || 'b := 0 || 'n := 5}
*** where L2  = save(save(layout, $ledger $nonce, 5), $ledger $balances 1, 11)
***       SRC = save(save(save(mtSt, $balances, mapOf(0)), $nonce, 5), $balances 1, 11)

*** ---- B  the claim, written out and checked -------------------------------
*** StorageCopyMapping.maude's B, arrow for arrow, with `layout` standing for
*** the two decls it reduces to.
red in PAPER-FLAG-STEPS :
|- { storage := layout }
   < $ledger . $nonce = # 5 ;  $ledger . $balances [ # 1 ] = # 11 ;
     $ledger2 = $ledger ;
     var 'n = $ledger2 . $nonce ;
     var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >
    ~*> { storage := save(save(layout, $ledger $nonce, 5),
                    $ledger $balances 1, 11) }
        < $ledger2 = $ledger ;
          var 'n = $ledger2 . $nonce ;
          var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >
    ~>  { storage := save(save(save(layout, $ledger $nonce, 5),
                    $ledger $balances 1, 11),
                    $ledger2, save(save(save(mtSt, $balances, mapOf(0)),
                    $nonce, 5), $balances 1, 11)) }
        < var 'n = $ledger2 . $nonce ;
          var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >
    ~>  { storage := save(save(save(layout, $ledger $nonce, 5),
                    $ledger $balances 1, 11),
                    $ledger2, save(save(save(mtSt, $balances, mapOf(0)),
                    $nonce, 5), $balances 1, 11))
          || 'n := 5 }
        < var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >
    ~>  { storage := save(save(save(layout, $ledger $nonce, 5),
                    $ledger $balances 1, 11),
                    $ledger2, save(save(save(mtSt, $balances, mapOf(0)),
                    $nonce, 5), $balances 1, 11))
          || 'n := 5 || 'b := 0 } .                                        *** true

*** the negation, as in StorageCopyMapping.maude: 'b did NOT come from the
*** source. Not derivable here either.
red in PAPER-FLAG-STEPS :
|- { storage := layout }
   < $ledger . $nonce = # 5 ;  $ledger . $balances [ # 1 ] = # 11 ;
     $ledger2 = $ledger ;
     var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >
    ~*> { storage := save(save(save(layout, $ledger $nonce, 5),
                    $ledger $balances 1, 11),
                    $ledger2, save(save(save(mtSt, $balances, mapOf(0)),
                    $nonce, 5), $balances 1, 11))
          || 'b := 11 } .                                                  *** false

*** and the one-shot triple. A triple carries its entry storage in Hoare.maude's
*** own bracket, `[ st ]< … >(phi)`, not in a Steps.maude update: the two
*** notations mean the same thing here -- `fill` takes the entry snapshot from
*** `storage :=` precisely so that they do.
red in PAPER-FLAG-STEPS :
[ layout ]
< $ledger . $nonce = # 5 ;  $ledger . $balances [ # 1 ] = # 11 ;
  $ledger2 = $ledger ;
  var 'n = $ledger2 . $nonce ;
  var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >
    ('n == # 5 && 'b == # 0) .                                             *** true

*** ---- C  the same program with NO layout ----------------------------------
*** Drop the initial update and the model is told nothing about where the
*** mappings are. It is an untyped store, so the only evidence left is the
*** VALUE at the path, and an unwritten target has none: the copy leaf reads
*** the member off the source and 'b := 11 -- the mapping IS copied, which is
*** wrong for Solidity. This is ../../../StorageCompare.maude's 2d, reached
*** from the statement level instead of from a storage term.
***
*** It is not a defect of the model but the price of its input: map-ness lives
*** in the store, so a store that was never declared does not have it. The
*** sorted model cannot go wrong this way and cannot be told anything either --
*** its answer is fixed when $balances is declared at sort MapField. What each
*** model needs in order to be right is the whole difference between them.
red in PAPER-FLAG-STEPS :
run(< $ledger . $nonce = # 5 ;  $ledger . $balances [ # 1 ] = # 11 ;
      $ledger2 = $ledger ;
      var 'n = $ledger2 . $nonce ;
      var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >) .
*** {storage := save(save(save(mtSt, $ledger $nonce, 5), $ledger $balances 1,
*** 11), $ledger2, save(save(mtSt, $nonce, 5), $balances 1, 11))
***  || 'b := 11 || 'n := 5}
red in PAPER-FLAG-STEPS :
< $ledger . $nonce = # 5 ;  $ledger . $balances [ # 1 ] = # 11 ;
  $ledger2 = $ledger ;
  var 'b = $ledger2 . $balances [ # 1 ] ; >
    ('b == # 11) .                                    *** true -- the undeclared store
